  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 AM EDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: Midlevel longwave troughing continues to build \nacross North America today with its axis centered over the Great \nLakes Region. As a result, cool and dry cP air begins to influence \nthe northwoods today as temperatures fall back to climatological \nnorms for early October. Cold air advection will support highs in \nthe 60s while temperatures fall around freezing levels tonight. \nContinued surface high pressure will keep the remainder of the \nforecast period mostly dry besides a brief chance of isolated \nrainfall generated by an embedded shortwave pivoting  across the \nU.S./Canadian border. Additional overnight lows near freezing will \ncontinue frost/freeze concerns, especially Monday night.  \n\nForecast Details:Today and Saturday: Model soundings depict \nconditions quickly drying at the surface and aloft as the cold front \nmakes its way across Northern Michigan. Some lingering moisture \naround 5000ft AGL will generate some cloud cover while 850mb \ntemperatures continue to fall near zero. Widespread overnight lows \nin the mid/low 30s will generate frost concerns across the northern \nlower peninsula while interior eastern upper will likely experience \nbelow-freezing temperatures.  \n\nSunday through Thursday Outlook: The aforementioned weak embedded \nwave develops lee of the Canadian Rockies and works its way to the \nGreat Lakes Region this Sunday. Surface low pressure will make its \nway across Southern Canada and an additional frontal boundary will \npush enough moisture and lift across Northern Michigan to create \nisolated showers. Showers remain confined mainly to eastern upper \nwith only a general tenth of an inch or less of total rain for most \nareas.  Another round of post-frontal cold air advection will lower \novernight temperatures to at or below freezing for interior northern \nlower. \n\nLongwave midlevel troughing progresses downstream this Monday and \ngets replaced by shortwave ridging. Subsidence aloft will keep \nconditions dry through the remainder of the forecast period with \nhighs near normal for early October.",
